\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Illustration of open set (right), closed set (left), and neither open nor closed (middle) in the plane $\mathbb  {R}^2$. The dashed lines indicate boundaries that are \emph  {not} included in the set, while the full lines indicate boundaries that \emph  {are} in the set. The rightmost set is open, since for every point in the set there is a finite distance to the edge. The leftmost set includes the boundary. If we select a point on the boundary, any $\varepsilon $-ball will be partially outside the set. On the other hand, the complement of the set is open, since it has no boundary! The middle set contains parts of its boundary, but not all, so it cannot be closed.}}{40}{figure.3.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:open-closed}{{3.2}{40}{Illustration of open set (right), closed set (left), and neither open nor closed (middle) in the plane $\RR ^2$. The dashed lines indicate boundaries that are \emph {not} included in the set, while the full lines indicate boundaries that \emph {are} in the set. The rightmost set is open, since for every point in the set there is a finite distance to the edge. The leftmost set includes the boundary. If we select a point on the boundary, any $\epsilon $-ball will be partially outside the set. On the other hand, the complement of the set is open, since it has no boundary! The middle set contains parts of its boundary, but not all, so it cannot be closed}{figure.3.2}{}}
